Introduction
The wake generated by a helicopter blade is characterized by concentrated tip vortices. These tip vortices are typically strong enough to persist for many rotor revolutions, and the resulting flow field is highly unsteady. The formation and evolution of the tip vortices is highly dependent on the blade loading. In turn, the velocities induced by the wake significantly affect the blade loading and the flapping motion of the blades. Therefore, accurate modeling of the wake and its interaction with the rotor system is critical to the successful prediction of overall rotor aerodynamics and rotor performance.
