Traditional rotor wake modeling efforts have primarily involved the use of free-wake methods (Refs. 1–6). These are potential flow based methods, which are relatively inexpensive and can often provide useful predictions and insight, but rely on empirical inputs. For instance, the free wake model of Bhagwat and Leishman (Ref. 4) assumes a vortex filament representation of the wake, and hence, relies on empiricism for the vortex release locations, initial vortex strengths, core structure, and viscous and turbulent diffusion. The Reynolds-averaged Navier–Stokes (RANS) equations currently represent the highest level of fluid dynamic approximation that can be utilized in the complete numerical simulation of flow at Reynolds numbers relevant to fixed- and rotary-wing applications. With the development of computational power over the past two decades, these simulations have been applied in the solution of practical rotor problems. Most of this research has, however, concentrated on on-surface load distributions. For rotary-wings, most comprehensive analyses use a prescribed/Lagrangian wake model to simulate the tip vortex induced inflow (Ref. 7). More recently, overset meshes (Refs. 8–10) have been used to “capture” the wake instead of modeling the same. Even though these calculations showed reasonable comparison of integrated coefficients (and sometimes surface pressures) with experimental/flight test data, only the “overall” effects of the wake were captured because of excessive numerical dissipation. Thus, the internal structure of the tip vortex is not addressed. The authors are not aware of any publication in which a detailed comparison of the vortex structure with experimental data has been attempted beyond a downstream distance of three chords from the trailing edge. The primary objective of this work is to use improved algorithms to reduce numerical and turbulence modeling errors, and to evaluate the methodology for accurately calculating the vortex wake structure. An additional objective is to keep the computational cost to a minimum. The methodology will be validated with the following experimental data sets:
