Background
A successful methodology for the detailed prediction of vortex structures has proved to be elusive because of a variety of issues. These include numerical errors, grid requirements, and RANS closure.
Numerical errors
Even if one assumes that the governing equations exactly model the physical phenomena, the numerical solution is bound to generate errors. In the case of time dependent partial differential equations like the Euler and Navier–Stokes equations, discretization errors can be viewed (in a linear sense) as arising from numerical dispersion and numerical dissipation (Ref. 13). Numerical dispersion causes waves of different wave lengths to propagate at different speeds, and can result in spurious oscillations in high gradient regions and shocks. Numerical dissipation acts as an artifical diffusion mechanism and is the primary factor that causes inaccuracies in most tip vortex calculations. Unlike non-linear features like shocks, tip vortices have no re-steepening mechanisms, and hence artificial decay of the vortex will result in an irrecoverable loss of information. Typically, the use of high-order accurate schemes is essential to reduce numerical errors in tip vortex simulations (Ref. 14).
Grid requirements
The aforementioned numerical errors can be reduced by using highorder accurate numerical schemes in conjunction with adequate mesh resolution. In RANS-based tip vortex simulations, two different types of grid spacings are required: 1) In the near-wall region, the normal distance between a point on the surface and the first off-surface point should be roughly one wall
unit (ν/√τw/ρ), and the stretching in the normal direction should be small. This is required to accurately represent the wall shear stress in the laminar sub-layer and the buffer layers (Ref. 15). For a blade at a chord based Re ≈ 1 × 106, a wall unit would correspond to a physical spacing of ≈5 × 10−6c. In addition, the spacing in the non-normal direction can be orders of magnitude larger. 2) In the wake region, a fine mesh spacing is required to resolve the tip vortex. Based on inviscid computations on an isolated tip vortex, it is estimated that (Ref. 14) at least 15 points are required across the vortical core. In addition, studies (Refs. 14, 16) have also hinted at the importance of maintaining reasonable cell aspect ratios (to account for features that are non-orthogonal to grid planes), thus imposing stringent resolution requirements in all three directions. In practical computations, a compromise has to be achieved between maintaining the required resolution, while keeping the overall computational cost manageable.
RANS closure
The averaging of the Navier–Stokes equations introduces the need for turbulence models. The most popularly used closure models are the linear eddy viscosity models like k − ε (Ref. 17), Spalart–Allmaras (Ref. 18), etc. In this approach, the Reynolds stress is related to the mean strain rates assuming isotropy. Though simple, this has turned out to be the only feasible solution to turbulence modeling for complex flows and
configurations. One of the deficiencies of linear eddy viscosity closures is that these models cannot implicitly account for effects like streamline curvature and solid body rotation, which are known to be significant in tip vortex flows. On the other hand, more complex RANS closures like the so-called Second Moment Closure (SMC) (Ref. 19) models can naturally account for these effects because Reynolds stresses are inherently related to flow gradients. Even though the SMC models appear to be physically appealing and have been shown to successfully model representative problems (Ref. 20), consistent superiority of these models has not been established for complex problems (Ref. 21). In addition, practical implementation of these models is known to be difficult because of their inherent complexity and lack of numerical robustness (Ref. 22). It has to be mentioned that in addition to the aforementioned issues, development and validation of computational methodologies suffer from a dearth of reliable experimental reference data on tip vortex evolution. While the far-field vortex measurements are affected by vortex instabilities and aperiodicity (Refs. 11, 23, 24), near-field studies are complicated by the fact that the flow gradients and turbulence intensities are large. In the present work, the use of proper grid resolution (by means of grid clustering and overset meshes) and high-order accurate numerical schemes are used to minimize numerical errors. A simple correction to the production term of the turbulence model to account for rotational effects is seen to provide reliable predictions.
Computational Methodology
Computations were performed using a modified version of the compressible RANS code TURNS (Ref. 25). The inviscid terms were computed using the third/fifth order WENO schemes (Ref. 26) with Roe’s flux difference splitting. The viscous terms were computed using the second/fourth order central differencing. The Spalart–Allmaras (Ref. 18) turbulence model was used to close the RANS equations. The equations were solved in the rotating reference frame, and hence a steadystate solution was sought. The convergence of the flow solution was assessed by at least 3.5 orders of magnitude of reduction in flow solver and turbulence residuals, after which the run was stopped when the total thrust started to fluctuate (at a small amplitude) around a mean state. Many experimental (Refs. 23, 27) and analytical (Ref. 28) studies on tip vortices have reported largely reduced turbulence levels in the vortical core. This has been attributed to the near solid body rotation that prevails in the core. It is known (Ref. 20) that linear eddy viscosity closures cannot model this behavior implicitly. To accurately represent the physics, one has to ensure that the turbulence model does indeed replicate this phenomena. The Spalart–Allmaras model bases the production term on the magnitude of the vorticity vector. This is known to perform well for wall-bounded flows. However, the core region of the tip vortex is highly vortical and axisymmetrically strained, but as mentioned earlier suppresses turbulence. The S–A model uses a production term, which in the wake region reduces to
P = cb1 Sνt , (1)
where cb1 = 0.1355 and νt is the eddy-viscosity function. Computations were performed with S = |ω| and S = |ω| + 2 min(0, |D| − |ω|), where |ω| and |D| are the magnitudes of the vorticity and strain tensors, respectively. The former was suggested in the original paper (Ref. 18); the latter correction was first used in Ref. 29 and attempts to suppress the turbulent production in regions where the vorticity is much larger than the strain rate (like tip vortices). The modification will be passive in thin shear layers where the magnitudes of the strain rate and vorticity are roughly equivalent.


