Experimental configuration
The test case chosen is the experiment of Martin et al. (Ref. 11). A single untwisted blade with rectangular planform (with a square tip shape), and NACA 2415 cross-section was used. The relevant test case details are given in Table 1. As mentioned earlier, accurate measurement of the vortex velocity profiles especially in the far-field is difficult because of the uncertainties associated with the aperiodicity of the vortex (Ref. 30). Hence, the measured profiles have to be corrected. The present test case was corrected by Martin et al. (Ref. 11) using the method described in Ref. 31.
Grid system
For the computations, a three-grid system was used. For the blade grid, a body-conforming C–O mesh of dimension 161 × 55 × 48 (≈0.43 million points) in the streamwise, spanwise and normal directions, respectively, was used. Since the flow field of a hovering rotor is cylindrical, the spanwise sections of this mesh are roughly aligned with the local flow direction in the wake region. Compared to a non-rotated mesh, the computed vortex profiles were found to be much less dissipative as a result of rough alignment with the vortex axis. The grid extended to a normal distance of slightly more than 2 chord lengths, and the normal spacing at the wall is 1 × 10−5c (<1 wall unit). A refined overset grid of dimension 135 × 176 × 101 (≈2.4 million points) was used to resolve the tip vortex (Fig. 1). This grid starts at the trailing edge of the rotor and extends to a wake age of around 330◦. Each streamwise section (176 × 101) of this grid is equi-spaced, consisting of square cells of side 0.01c. In the axial direction, hyperbolic tangent stretching was used. In the high gradient regions, (0◦ < ψ ≤ 45◦) the axial spacing ranged from 0.5◦ ≤ ψ ≤ 2◦. Beyond this region, the spacing ranged from 2◦ ≤ ψ ≤ 6◦. The placement and alignment of this grid was based on the predicted wake geometry given by Landgrebe’s model (Ref. 32). To account for the far-field boundaries, a cylindrical background mesh of size 181 × 50 × 60 (≈0.54 million points) in the streamwise, spanwise and normal directions, respectively, was utilized as shown in Fig. 1(b). The radius of the outer boundary of this grid is twice the rotor radius, and the top and bottom boundaries are one rotor radius and two rotor radii away from the blade surface, respectively. Figure 1(b) also shows that the part of the background grid that overlaps with the blade grid is hole-cut. At the outer boundaries of the background grid, the point-sink/momentum theory characteristic boundary condition (Ref. 25) was used. The outer boundaries in the current calculations are relatively close to the blade surface as compared to that normally used in the literature. This could potentially affect the rotor loading. For instance, Ahmad and Strawn (Ref. 9) show that for a 4-bladed rotor case, the solution was insensitive to change in boundary location for boundary distances beyond three radii from the blade surface. Shorter distances changed the computed thrust levels by a few percent. It has to be mentioned however, that the thrust
