Numerical results
Figure 2 shows the vorticity magnitude along selected azimuthal planes in the overset vortex grid. It is evident that by a wake age of 25◦, the vortex has rolled up completely, and the magnitude of the vorticity in the tip vortex is very much higher than that in the wake. Also, the vortex structure seems to have approached axisymmetry by the 25◦ wake age. Overall, the vorticity in the tip vortex diffuses at a much slower rate than the vorticity in the trailing vortex sheet, a result that can be attributed to the curvature induced stabilizing effects. Figures 3 and 4 compare the computed peak swirl velocity and core radius with experiments as a function of the wake age. The third-order scheme performs poorly compared to the fifth-order scheme, suggesting non-optimal spatial discretization that causes increased numerical dissipation. While idealized tests (Ref. 14) have indicated that around 15 points are required across the core to effectively eliminate discretization errors, the current computation uses only 7 points in the initial wake ages (increasing to around 20 at later wake ages). To study the effect of increased resolution, the original overset vortex grid was replaced by a refined grid (extending to a wake-age of 45◦) that was 1.5 times as fine in the cross-stream directions. This did not


