Two-Bladed Hovering Rotor
In the previous section, computations were performed on a singlebladed hovering rotor, with significant emphasis on the vortex velocity profiles. In this section, simulations will be performed on the two-bladed experimental setup of Caradonna and Tung (Ref. 12). This test case was chosen because it provides detailed blade surface pressure measurements and vortex trajectory data. This test case has also been previously validated using the Euler and RANS equations by several researchers (Refs. 24, 34, 35).
Experimental configuration
The experimental setup consisted of a two-bladed rigid rotor in a hover chamber. The blades were of a rectangular planform, and were untwisted with a radius of 1.143 m. The aspect ratio of the blade was 6. The blades used a NACA 0012 airfoil section along the entire span. A pre-cone angle of 0.5◦ was set for the blades. Of the different experiments conducted, Table 2 shows the cases that were chosen for validation in the present study. Of particular interest, and one that poses a challenge, is the 5◦ collective case. The associated thrust level is relatively low, causing the following blade to pass very close to the vortex. Accurate resolution of this phenomena could be critical in the prediction of hover performance.


