Conclusions
Highly accurate computation of the wake system is imperative in the design of efficient helicopters. The RANS equations represent the highest level of fluid dynamic approximation that is currently feasible in the simulation of high Reynolds number flows. However, practical RANS simulations of tip vortex formation and evolution suffer from 1) numerical diffusion, which arises primarily from inaccuracies in the discretization of the convective term in the NS equations, and 2) inaccurate turbulence modeling, which is a result of the unavailability of a perfect closure model to the RANS equations. In the present work, the error from numerical diffusion was reduced by the use of proper grid resolution (by means of grid clustering and overset meshes) and high-order accurate numerical schemes. None of the available turbulence models are accurate or efficient enough to completely describe the complex tip vortex flow field, but reliable solutions can be obtained by adding a simple rotational correction to the production term in the Spalart–Allmaras turbulence model. For the first time, an attempt has been made at detailed validation of the vortex structure trailed from rotating blades. The following are the specific conclusions noted from this study: 1) The use of a refined overset mesh, high-order schemes and turbulence model modification was seen to be essential to accurately resolve the evolving tip vortex. 2) For the computations on the single-bladed rotor, fair agreement was achieved with the experimental swirl velocity measurements up to 300◦ of wake evolution behind the trailing edge. 3) For the two-bladed rotor, the tip vortex could be tracked up to four wake passages behind the trailing edge. Good correlation was observed with experimental surface pressure measurements and vortex trajectory data. 4) With the use of appropriately refined meshes, the interaction of the blade with the first vortex passage was found to be well represented. Taken together, the above set of reference data represent a spectrum of validations that establish the fidelity of the methodology in predicting hovering rotor flow fields in an efficient manner. There is however, wide scope for improvement and avenues for future work. A primary suggestion for the continuation of this work would be conduct a more rigorous grid convergence study. Advanced turbulence models such as the Second Moment Closure models (Ref. 19) can be used to better represent curvature and anisotropic effects. Application of the current methodology to more realistic hovering rotors would require slightly different mesh topologies, for instance, to resolve the inboard vortex sheet.
